  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front brings a round of beneficial rain \nshowers through the first half of today, followed by dry weather and \nseasonable temperatures this weekend.\n\nA mid level trough digging across the upper Great Lakes is forcing a \ncold front southeast through western and northcentral NY this \nmorning, with the main area of anafrontal rain showers slowly \nsagging southeastward into the area. Ahead of the front, PWAT values \nare near the 100th percentile for this time of year, along with a \nstout 35-45 knot southwesterly LLJ. However, with the showers being \nmainly anafrontal in nature, this activity will not be able to take \nfull advantage of the more plentiful moisture and much stronger \nforcing for ascent along and ahead of the low level portion of the \nfront. Thus, while there will be a period of beneficial showers, the \nheavy rainfall/flooding threat will be near zero. The main window \nfor more persistent showers will be this morning through \nmidday/early afternoon, just behind the surface cold front. Rainfall \namounts of a half to three-quarters of an inch are expected across a \ngood chunk of the area, with lesser amounts expected across the \ninterior Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and southern Tug Hill region. \nA few widely scattered light showers may linger in the wake of the \ncold front this afternoon, especially toward the North Country, \notherwise dry and seasonable weather is on tap for the upcoming \nweekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures with frost potential \nearly next week.\n\nA few showers may accompany a strong cold front forecast to cross \nthe area Sunday night, ushering in the coolest airmass we've seen \nsince last spring. 850 mb temperatures are expected to plunge to \nnear 0C to -2C during the first part of next week, with daytime \nhighs not climbing out of the 50's for much of the region, and \npossibly only upper 40's for highs across the Tug Hill and western \nDacks by Tuesday.\n \nThe axis of a strong upper level trough axis will cross the area \nMonday, possibly producing a few additional showers, otherwise \nmainly dry weather is expected. The main impact will be frost \npotential Monday night, and possibly again Tuesday night, especially \ninland of the lakeshores.",
